    "measurementTechnique": [
      "For a full description of the methods, see associated manuscript. Briefly, shallow riverbed sediments (1\u20133 cm) and filtered river water were collected at WHONDRS sites across the contiguous United States between April 2022 and October 2023 and shipped to Pacific Northwest National Laboratory. In the laboratory, homogenized sediments from each site were either allowed to dry (dry treatment) or kept inundated (wet treatment) for 21-days, followed by re-inundation with native water to quantify sediment oxygen (O\u2082) consumption rates using optical oxygen sensors. Dissolved organic matter (DOM) chemistry of post-incubation water was analyzed using solid-phase extraction and ultrahigh-resolution Fourier transform ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometry (FTICR-MS). Molecular formulas were processed with CoreMS and ftmsRanalysis to calculate DOM thermodynamic metrics (Gibbs free energy of carbon oxidation, \u0394Gcox, and thermodynamic efficiency, \u03bb) using unique molecular formulas within the wet and dry treatments at each site. Sediment physical and geochemical properties and watershed-scale climatic and land-cover variables were combined with DOM thermodynamics and oxygen consumption data and used in clustering, generalized additive models, and breakpoint analyses to evaluate how moisture modulates thermodynamic regulation of sediment respiration.",
      "The processed data in this package were generated from data archived separately in ESS-DIVE. Oxygen consumption rates available in Laan et al. (2024) were derived from dissolved oxygen time series measured with optode-based batch reactors, using segmented regression and diagnostic tests to identify the most linear portion of each incubation and estimate slopes. FTICR-MS data were processed using CoreMS to calibrate spectra and assign molecular formulas, followed by ftmsRanalysis to compute molecular properties and classify compounds. DOM thermodynamic metrics (\u0394Gcox and \u03bb) were calculated from unique molecular formulas within the wet and dry treatments after removing poorly calibrated samples and peaks associated with blanks. Site-level median values and treatment effect sizes were computed by aggregating replicate incubations, and hierarchical clustering was applied to DOM thermodynamic responses and within-sample environmental variables to define response groups. Large-scale watershed metrics (e.g., aridity, potential evapotranspiration, land cover) were pulled from existing geospatial products and joined to sites using watershed boundaries. Generalized additive models and breakpoint analysis were used to estimate a sediment moisture threshold at which DOM thermodynamic influence on oxygen consumption weaken. The tabular data in the \"Data\" folder reflect these processed and aggregated products; the \"src\" folder contains R scripts that implement the full workflow from raw inputs to the analysis-ready tables and figures distributed here."
    ],
